    "bigger_question_one_sentence": "Do the variants carrying shared depression-gastrointestinal liability act through gut tissue regulation rather than brain tissue regulation?"
  },
  "controls": {
    "positive_control": "Recovery of well-established depression loci (the chromosome 1 NEGR1 region and the DRD2 dopamine-receptor region, which the paper's own gene-set tables ST25-ST29 highlight) and of the major histocompatibility complex region for the immune factor.",
    "negative_control": "Shuffle the Z values within each linkage-disequilibrium block and confirm collapse to the allele-frequency-bin dummy; also train on minor allele frequency and gene density alone to see how much of the apparent signal is confounding.",
    "external_validation": "An independently published depression GWAS not among the 31 inputs, plus the paired no-QSNP versions of each factor file."
  },
  "honest_claim_h3": "Among European-ancestry common variants on genome build GRCh37, sequence-derived annotations rank variants by their association with a genomic structural equation model composite of depression and physical-disease GWAS, conditional on linkage disequilibrium and allele frequency, and only for variants that survived the paper's heterogeneity (QSNP) filter.",
  "plain_idea": {
    "one_liner": "Use the DNA sequence around a common variant to predict how strongly it carries shared depression and physical-disease genetic risk.",
    "train_on": "Per-variant rows from four genome-wide files, one for each disease cluster (cardiovascular, metabolic, gastrointestinal, immune), roughly 6.8 million variants each according to supplementary table ST14. The files supply only coordinates and alleles, so all features would be built from an external reference genome and annotation sets.",
    "predict": "The Z statistic measuring each variant's association with the shared depression-disease latent factor. That number is not a new measurement: it is produced by genomic structural equation modelling, a statistical reweighting of 31 previously published GWAS.",
    "why_worthwhile": "Psychiatric-genetics groups would get a sharper shortlist of candidate causal variants at the gut-brain-axis loci the paper highlights; even a failure would quantify how little of this composite signal is readable from sequence."
  },
  "paper_type": "computational",
  "crowded_field_case": {
    "more_or_better_data": {
      "answer": "no",
      "why": "The four factor files are reweighted combinations of 31 already-public GWAS, so they add no new measurements. Enformer, AlphaGenome and PolyFun were developed against far broader data (thousands of functional genome profiles, dozens of UK Biobank traits)."
    },
    "confidence_score": {
      "answer": "partly",
      "why": "The standard error column gives a per-variant precision estimate, and the QSNP versus no-QSNP file pairs give a sensitivity handle. But existing fine-mapping methods already output calibrated posterior inclusion probabilities, which is a stronger confidence statement than anything these files support."
    },
    "models_beatable": {
      "answer": "no",
      "why": "AlphaGenome (2025-2026) and Enformer (2021) are recent, evaluated on held-out genomic regions, and PolyFun is evaluated with proper linkage-disequilibrium blocks. These are honest evaluations by specialist groups; a laptop-scale model is not going to beat them."
    },
    "guaranteed_improvement": {
      "answer": "no",
      "why": "The condition that fails is baseline access: AlphaGenome cannot be run at genome scale on a CPU-only laptop, and the published PolyFun numbers are on different traits and splits, so there is no shared evaluation to beat."
    }
  },
  "label_elsewhere": {
