  "leakage_plan": null,
  "success_criteria": null,
  "publication_target": null,
  "pitch": null,
  "ml_idea": "Predict each common variant's association statistic (Z) for the shared depression-physical disease latent factors from DNA sequence context and functional annotation, with leave-one-chromosome-out or leave-one-linkage-disequilibrium-block-out evaluation, then test whether a model fitted on one disease cluster transfers to another. Rejected because the features must be built entirely from external genome-scale resources, the effective evaluation set is ~1,019 independent loci rather than 6.8 million rows, and the competitors (PolyFun, Enformer, AlphaGenome) are open, recent and already honestly evaluated.",
  "effort": {
    "hours_to_feature_table": 35,
    "working_set_gb": 3.0,
    "needs_gpu": true,
    "peak_ram_gb": 6.0,
    "embedding_hours": 100.0,
    "cv_run_minutes": 30,
    "compute_note": "Estimated, not timed. Working set from the verified figshare file sizes (227-318 MB gzipped each, four factors) expanding roughly threefold on decompression; peak RAM assumes streaming by chromosome with pandas chunking. Embedding hours assume roughly 1 million 2 kb windows at about 3 windows per second on CPU, which is why the backbone route needs a GPU or a heavy subset.",
    "cheapest_killing_check": "Count the independent loci per factor before anything else. I did this: 172, 537, 170 and 140 rows in supplementary tables ST15-ST18, 1,019 total. That is the real number of positive examples, and it kills the idea that this is a 6.8-million-row dataset."
  },
  "verdict": "reject",
  "verdict_reason": "Part A passes cleanly: the four factor files are open under CC BY 4.0 on figshare, the target (beta, SE, P, Z) is already a column, every column and the genome build are documented in a readme I read, and every count I could check matched the paper exactly (172/537/170/140 loci). It fails further down. Part C scores 5/10 and Part I scores 3/8. The files supply labels only, so every feature must be assembled from external genome-scale resources; the effective evaluation unit is the linkage-disequilibrium block, so the honest sample size is about 1,019 loci rather than 6.8 million variants; the label is a deterministic reweighting of 31 published GWAS, so any feature touching those GWAS leaks by construction; and the task of ranking variants from sequence and annotation is served by open, recent, honestly evaluated methods (PolyFun, Enformer, AlphaGenome, SBayesRC) that cannot be run as a laptop baseline, meaning the project could only succeed by beating numbers we cannot reproduce. This is also exactly the genomics-scale, European-ancestry-only resource the project brief deprioritises, and the angle is not strong enough to override that."
}
